	source S63 {
		title.value History of João Neiva, Espírito Santo, Brazil
		author Getúlio Vargas Foundation
		note {
			text """
Verbatim text: https://www.joaoneiva.es.gov.br/v1/?page=conteudo&subfrom=MUNIC%C3%8DPIO&pagina=05e735a3b2

With the arrival of the first families arise the villages of Acioli (1887) and Demétrio Ribeiro (1891).
At the beginning of the century, the engineer and federal deputy from Bahia, João Augusto Neiva, fought hard in the Federal Chamber for the installation of the Diamantina Railroad, belonging to Companhia Estrada de Ferro Vitória x Minas.
With the installation of the Railroad, the Railway Station was inaugurated, inaugurated on December 20, 1905. The land for the work was donated by Mr. Negri Orestes.
Pedro Nolasco, who was the founder of the construction of the Railroad, to honor the federal deputy João Augusto Neiva, gave the Station the name of João Neiva. It is around the Station that the village "João Neiva" appears.
On December 30, 1921, João Neiva, through Law no. 1305 is elevated to District. After several attempts at emancipation, finally on March 30, 1988, the Legislative Assembly met again, to confirm the result of the plebiscite, confirming the same. On that day, the Deputy Governor of the State Carlos Alberto Batista da Cunha, temporarily acting as Governor of the State, signed Law No. 4076 published in the Official Gazette of May 11, 1988, creating the Municipality of João Neiva.
On November 15, 1988, the first election was held in the Municipality. Having been elected Mayor, Aluyzio Morelatto, for Vice Mayor, José Anízio Ivo Secomandi, and for the first City Council the Aldermen: Alécio Jocimar Fávaro, Antônio Pandolfi, Augusto Tessarolo, Ayrton Fornaciari, Edson Luiz Dal Piaz Coutinho, José Carlos Alves José Domingos Del Pupo, José Francisco Grippa, José Geraldo Colombo, Jurandir Mattos do Nascimento, Maria Luiza Casoti Louzada, Natalino Antonio Gasparine and Waldemar de Barros.
With the inauguration of those elected on November 15, 1988, the Municipality was officially installed on January 1, 1989, at a ceremony held at the João Neiva Community Center, presided over by Dr. Machado de Souza, Judge of the Comarca de Ibiraçu .

COLONIZATION
The colonization of the region where the municipality of João Neiva is located began with the arrival of immigrants, mainly Italians. The first families came in the year 1877. But the great levies took a little longer to arrive.
On February 24, 1874 the ship "Sofia" anchored in the Port of Vitória. The Tyroleses were brought in by Mr. Pietro Tabachi, who had signed a contract.
The immigrants who were destined to the colonial nuclei of Santa Cruz (Conde d'Eu), Acioli de Vasconcelos and Demétrio Ribeiro, which today constitute the present municipalities of Ibiraçu and João Neiva, disembarked in Vitória, followed later in smaller vapors to Santa Cruz . From there they climbed in canoes, the Piraqueaçu River to the Port of Santana, in Córrego Fundo, from where they continued, on foot, to the various sessions of these nuclei.
The foundation of the colonial nuclei Acioli de Vasconcelos and Antônio Prado in 1887, Demétrio Ribeiro in 1891 and Moniz Freire in 1894 had the objective of resolving the ancient and important problem of the colonization of the Rio Doce, already tried so often and always with failure.
Dr. Antônio Francisco de Atayde was the founder of the Colonial Nucleus Acioli de Vasconcelos, and the name given to the Nucleus is a tribute to the then Inspector General of Land and Colonization, lieutenant colonel Francisco de Barros and Acioli de Vasconcelos, veteran of the War of the Paraguay.
Officially, however, the first establishments of settlers in the colonial nucleus, later named Acioli de Vasconcelos, were effected by the engineer himself Francisco de Atayde, from August of 1888.
However, the largest contingents of Italian immigrants established in the Colonial Acioli de Vasconcelos Nucleus took place during the months of January, September and October of 1889 and beginning of 1892.
Most of these immigrants arrived in Vitória aboard the steamer "Estrela", on March 29, 1889, from Rio de Janeiro, and some in the "Adria", directly from Genoa - Italy, on December 27, 1888 .
Bibliographical data of Lieutenant-Colonel Acioli de Vasconcelos: the name of the Colonial Nucleus Acioli de Vasconcelos was given in honor of Lieutenant-Colonel Francisco de Barros and Acioli de Vasconcelos, who held the position of General Inspector of Land and Colonization at the time of the foundation of this nucleus.
Acioli de Vasconcelos, died on September 26, 1907, before turning 61 in Rio de Janeiro, and the "Diário da Manhã", of this Capital, on October 9 of that year, published a note communicating his death and presenting some information about your life:
 - volunteer of the War of Paraguay;
 - participant in the battles of Estero-Belaco, Passo da Pátria, May 24, Avaí and Itororó;
 - appointed Secretary of the Navy Arsenal;
 - appointed as Head of Section of the Secretariat of State of the Ministry of Agriculture, Commerce and Public Works for his private friend, Visconde do Rio Branco;
 - appointed by the Minister Afonso Pena to assume the General Inspection of Lands and Colonization, where he had the opportunity to contribute to the good management of the immigration and colonization service in our country, and particularly in our State.

THE FIRST VICTIMS
As soon as they had been established in the Acioli Nucleus of Vasconcelos, the immigrants who arrived there in 1889, suffered serious illnesses, and some died soon in the first contacts with the new land.
These victims were, in large measure, unlucky enough to be established in the most unhealthy region of the nucleus, which comprised a vast expanse of land, between the mouth of Esperança Stream, on the Pau Gigante, to the vicinity of the Doce River, including Lagoa of Coffee.
It was a low, marshy, disease-infested region.
The colonization in this part of the nucleus was processed with sacrifice of many human lives and did not obtain the expected result.

OLD TEACHERS
 - Sebastiana Pereira Grillo, Djanira Ferraz Coutinho, Dina Pinheiro, Maria Bittencourt Rocha (Dona Cotinha), Francisca Eulália Santana (Dona Chiquinha), Fernandina das Neves Raizer and the sisters Adelaide and Olga Raizer, the latter two being the only ones born in Acioli.
 - Sebastiana Pereira Grillo, graduated in December 1916, and on February 7, 1917 was appointed to administer the School of the female of Acioli, and entered into exercise on 22 of the same month.

THE FIRST PHYSICIAN
One of the first doctors of the place was Dr. Francisco de Paula Amarante Filho, a native of Rio Grande do Sul.

THE FIRST TRADERS
One of the first to settle in Acioli was Coriolano Pereira, who arrived there in the first decade of this century, coming from Nova Almeida, and ended up becoming one of the most important people of the place.
Next, the following merchants were established: Manoel Alvarenga Pimentel, Narciso Machado, Francisco Campostrini and many others.

TRIUMPH BAR
The Colonial Acioli de Vasconcelos Nucleus was slowly establishing itself in its early years of foundation, mainly due to the region's unhealthiness, the precariousness of the roads and the lack of more assistance.
The first section of this nucleus to present development was Barra do Triunfo, where the farm founded by Giuseppe Battisti existed.
It acquired land in the Rio Ubás and Barra do Triunfo, devoting itself to agriculture (cultivating coffee, sugar cane and cereals, also assembling mills for the processing of these products), producing high quality beer, with monthly production of 3,000 thousand bottles, and to trade; he also acted as a carpenter. It was on this farm also that the inhabitants of the nucleus met on festive occasions.
With the transfer of Giuseppe Battisti to Pau Gigante, where he came to occupy important positions, including that of Mayor of the Municipality, the direction of business in Barra do Triunfo was under the responsibility of his brother Guglielmo Battisti, whose name was anointed for Guilherme Baptista , to avoid political persecution, of Getúlio Vargas' policy.
Guilherme Baptista achieved extraordinary development in the region.
He founded in 1907 the "Lira Triunfense" - music band, having as first master Mr. Antônio Barcelos.
In 1915, he founded the "Peasant" - soccer team, also participating as a player.
In the 1920s, Guilherme Baptista bought a truck and a car, the first to drive in the region, and at the same time built the road that connects Barra do Triunfo to Acioli.
Still in this decade, in partnership with his nephew Florencio, son of Giovanni Baptista, he built a dam on the Ubás River and inaugurated the Hydroelectric Plant that for several years, until the advent of Excelsa, provided light to Acioli and surroundings. It was also acquired the first radio of the place.
This successful Italian immigrant died on September 27, 1941, and was buried in Bar of Triumph, leaving a widow, Maria Casotti and the following children: Hilario, Hortêncio, Theodolinda, Maurílio, Euclides, Aldemira, Hermes, Eulália, Davina, Iracema and Euflodísio.

THE CHURCH OF OUR LADY OF CARAVAGGIO
The Church of Our Lady of Caravaggio, on the property of the family Ceccato, between Acioli and Barra do Triunfo, was built by Ceccato Giovanni Pietro and annually, on May 26, the day dedicated to this venerated saint, there is performed the beautiful and lively party, in his honor.
The Caravaggio sanctuary in Italy, where the pious young woman lived, is in the Diocese of Cremona, 80 km from Milan.

COLONIAL NÚCLEO DEMÉTRIO RIBEIRO
Founded in 1891, it consists of the following sections: Crubixá, Thirteen of June, Clotário, São Carlos, Alto Bergamo, São Benedito, Tranquilo and Treze de Maio.
The foundation of this nucleus was influenced by the immigrant Negri Orestes.
As for the name of the Nucleus, it was given in honor of the Minister of Agriculture, Commerce and Public Works, Demétrio Ribeiro, of the Provisional Government of the Republic, headed by Marshal Teodoro da Fonseca.
The nucleus had at that time a zinc-covered shed for immigrants' lodging and a house that served as an office and pharmacy, with accommodations for the doctor and that, besides these Government buildings, there were still four business houses, two bakeries, two blacksmiths, two water powered mills for coffee pillar and cane mill.
Its main crops were coffee, sugar cane, corn, rice and potatoes.
With the inauguration of the Vitória a Minas Railway Station in December 1905, which was only 6km from the village of Demétrio Ribeiro, the situation of this nucleus has improved considerably, due to the advantages offered by this new means of transportation.

INAUGURATION OF WATER AND LIGHT SERVICES
In August 1924, under the initiative and resources of Mr. Giuseppe Battisti, the Water Service was inaugurated in the village of Pau Gigante, whose improvement, as it could not fail to be, was received with great enthusiasm and satisfaction by the local population.
Less than two years later, on May 23, 1926, the Hydroelectric Plant was inaugurated in the same village.
This plant installed in Cachoeira do Barro was practically destroyed in the night of 17 to 18 November 1942, by a water spout, which was constituted in the greatest disaster recorded in all the history of this Municipality.
At that time, almost all the bridges and plantations were destroyed and many houses disappeared, dragged by the flood, causing the homelessness of many people and some deaths.
The trains, the only means of transport of the place, stopped to circulate once some rails were uprooted.
Mr. Ayrton Bonesi, Mayor of the Municipality, from 1942 to 1947, fought all his administration to rebuild the Municipality. However the problems were many and the resources available were insignificant.
It reopened the Santa Teresinha Hydroelectric Plant on October 6, 1946, shortly before leaving the City Hall.
Years later the Municipality happened to be served by Escelsa.

INAUGURATION OF THE HYDROELECTRIC POWER PLANT OF JOÃO NEIVA
At the initiative of the firm of the brothers NEGRI, the Hydroelectric Plant of João Neiva was inaugurated, on December 13, 1924.

SCHOOL - SENAI
In order to attend to the children of the railroad workers of the workshops of João Neiva, the Liceu Pedro Nolasco was founded, directed and financed by an Association of Railways.
In 1947 the works of the "SENAI RAILWAYS" School of Porto Velho and João Neiva were started, for the formation of craftsman of the workshops of the Road.
In 1949 the first school period began at the SENAI RAILWAY SCHOOL of João Neiva, which counted on the professional courses of Mechanical Turning, Caldeiraria and Ferraria. The duration of the course was three years. In the latter, the students took internships at the Locomotives workshops, where they would work after graduating.
On August 28, 1948, Cia Vale do Rio Doce founded the "TALMO LUIZ SILVA" TRAINING CENTER, with the purpose of training professionals to attend to the company itself, maintaining, through agreement, SENAI teaching standards.
In 1991, with the deactivation of CVRD's workshops in João Neiva, all the assets were donated to the João Neiva City Hall, creating the EDUCATIONAL FOUNDATION "Dr. HILDO GARCIA ", whose main objective was to maintain the" Talmo Luiz Silva "Training Center; the foundation was maintained by the Municipal Government of João Neiva, maintaining the same standards.
There was a disruption of the agreement with SENAI and the signing of the protocol of intentions with CEFET-ES (Federal Center for Technological Education of Espírito Santo) in 1999.
Currently, CEFORP is working to bring new courses and principally to implement technical vocational courses in the Municipality, providing the community with greater comfort and reliability in professional education in our region.

THE DEACTIVATION OF THE OFFICES OF JOÃO NEIVA
By not meeting the technical conditions demanded by the company, the workshops suffered several threats of deactivation through the ages. More than once the works of expansion and renovation were carried out, in order to adjust them to the new requirements. With the beginning of the export of ore in 1940, the increase in the number of trains and the weight of the cargo transported, the number of accidents increased, causing a workload to the workshops.
A major reform was imposed and began in 1943. However, after a few years, reforms have become obsolete. Despite the appeals and cries of the whole community, it was decided that the workshops of João Neiva would be deactivated; and on April 1, 1991, employees of João Neiva's workshops began their activities in the workshops located in the Tubarão port area of ​​Companhia Vale do Rio Doce, in Vitória.

THE FINAL APITHER
On July 25, 1991, the locomotive that came to João Neiva was totally weakened and rebuilt; the one that did so much for the benefit of Companhia Vale do Rio Doce.

BIOGRAPHY OF THE JOURNALIST AND DEPUTY JOÃO AUGUSTO NEIVA
João Augusto Neiva was born in the town of Barra do Rio Grande de São Francisco, in the current municipality of Barra (BA), on March 21, 1847, son of João Augusto Neiva and Joana Cândida de Castro. Of humble origin, he took the humanities course in Salvador and soon began to work. He started out as a newspaper typographer, but soon revealed a talent for reporter. In 1864 he became editor of The Constitutional, an organ of popularization of the Conservative Party; when it was replaced by the Jornal da Bahia, was kept in its functions. In September 1868, he was appointed to the Post Office by the Ministry of Transport and Public Works, and remained in office until 1879. During this period he was also administrator of the São João Theater.
In 1879 he was appointed officer of the secretariat of the Provincial Assembly of Bahia; was later promoted to general director, position in which he would retire in 1895. He initiated the political race electing himself provincial deputy by the 14th district for the legislature 1882-1883. With the implantation of the Republic he became a Federal Republican Party and became director of the Gazeta da Bahia. He was elected state deputy for the 1893-1894 legislature and was elected first secretary of the Legislative Assembly. After the breakup of the Republican Federalist Party, on April 15, 1894, he joined the Federal Republican Party, in the wing headed by the adviser Luís Viana, hence called the "Vianist."
Also in 1894 he was elected federal deputy, taking a seat in the Chamber of Deputies, in Rio de Janeiro, then Federal District. Several times re-elected, he would exercise successive mandates for 15 uninterrupted years, until 1908. In the Chamber of Deputies, he was part of the Finance Committee, among others. His conduct as a deputy, always behaving with loyalty, sincerity and kindness in the treatment, guaranteed the respect and admiration of the other members of the National Congress, in such a way that his influence surpassed the limits of the benches and overlapped that of the leaders of each one of them. This provoked in several leaders, mainly in the one of the Bahia bench, fear and distrust, since in more than one opportunity its orientations were disconsidered, preferring the parliamentarians to attend the personal request of João Neiva.
In spite of this prestige, in the election for the legislature 1909-1911 it failed to obtain the recognition of its mandate. Discontented with his compulsory departure, on October 6, 1909 deputies decided to appoint him head of the House Debates Section. On December 15, she was promoted to superintendent of the Mesa, position in which she retired. As a journalist he was also director of the newspapers Public Interest, Lighthouse, The Courier, and Telegraph. He was a meritorious partner of the Lyceum of Arts and Crafts of Bahia, and an honorary member of the Bahia Typographical Association. He died in Rio de Janeiro on August 21, 1923. He married Ana Adelaide Neiva with whom he had four children. Among them stood out Artur Neiva, who was noted as one of the greatest authorities in malaria, being the first to publish work on the disease;

